###### Abstract

J/\(\psi\) production in hadron collision contains plenty of physics, which have attracted a lot interests both in experimentally and theoretically. One of the main interest is its production mechanism, which have not been fully understood yet. The intuitive expectation of producing a J/\(\psi\) can be understand in terms of two distinct steps: the production of \(c\bar{c}\) pair, and the subsequent evolution of the \(c\bar{c}\) pair into the J/\(\psi\). Due to large mass of charm quark the process of producing a \(c\bar{c}\) pair can be calculated by perturbative Quantum Chromodynamics (pQCD), while the evolution of the \(c\bar{c}\) pair involves non-perurbative physics which can only be described by model. Different assumptions of the produced \(c\bar{c}\) in terms of the different quantum number and in terms of the different evolution process have led to various J/\(\psi\) models. The most notable ones among these models are the color-singlet model (CSM), the color-evaporation model (CEM), and the non-relativistic QCD (NRQCD) factorization approach. However, none of the existing theoretical models can simultaneously describe the transverse momentum (\(p_{T}\)) spectrum and the polarization. Furthermore, the composition of inclusive J/\(\psi\) is complicated, which include direct production via gluon fusion, parton fragmentation, and feed-down from excited charmonium states and B hadrons. Measurements of J/\(\psi\) production at a different beam energy can shed new lights on the understanding of J/\(\psi\) production mechanisms, and help to constrain model calculations.

On the other hand, the production of \(c\bar{c}\) could also be complicated. ALICE collaboration measured the charm production at central rapidity versus charged-particle multiplicity in proton-proton collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=7\) TeV. A faster than linear increase of the relative D meson yield as a function of charged-particle multiplicity at high multiplicity range is observed with no significant \(p_{T}\) dependence. By comparison of open charm and hidden charm's results, it concluded that the yield enhancement is related the \(c\bar{c}\) production process rather than the hadronisation process. Predictions from EPOS 3.099 with hydrodynamics and percolation model can both qualitative describe the data. However, the origin of the faster than linear increase is still not well understood. Measurement of J/\(\psi\) production as a function of charged-particle multiplicity at a very different collision energy of 500 GeV will help to understand the production and evolution of \(c\bar{c}\) pair at different energy.

In this thesis, we report measurements of J/\(\psi\) production in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=500\) GeV via di-electron channel at STAR. The data set used for the J/\(\psi\) reconstruction is triggered by Barrel Electromagnetic Calorimeter (BEMC) which requires the deposit energy inside the calorimeter above certain threshold. The trigger enriches the high\(p_{T}\) electron sample and also ensured the reconstructed vertex to be the correct vertex associated with a J/\(\psi\) in a high pile-up background. The particle identification of the trigger electrons is selected by the ionization energy loss (\(dE/dx\)) measured by the Time Projection Chamber (TPC) and the energy deposition in the BEMC. The other electrons with relative low transverse momenta are identified by \(dE/dx\) alone. By pairing the identified electron and positron, the J/\(\psi\) is reconstructed at high \(p_{T}\) (4 \(<\)\(p_{T}\) \(<\) 20 GeV/\(c\)) with a good signal to background ratio. The inclusive J/\(\psi\) cross section as a function of transverse momentum has a large kinematic range with 4 \(<\)\(p_{T}\)\(<\) 20 GeV/\(c\) at mid-rapidity. The NLO NRQCD prediction of the prompt J/\(\psi\) production is compared with the measurement and the prediction is consistent with our measurement. The J/\(\psi\)\(x_{T}\) scaling is also tested, our measurement also follow the \(x_{T}\) scaling with \(p_{T}\)\(\geq\) 4 GeV/\(c\). Feed-down contributions from \(\psi(2S)\) is studied in the \(p_{T}\) range of 4 \(<\)\(p_{T}\)\(<\) 12 GeV/\(c\), the measured \(\psi(2S)\) to J/\(\psi\) yield ratio via di-electron decay is 3.2 \(\pm\) 1%.

In addition, the J/\(\psi\) production as a function of charged-particle multiplicity is also measured, which shows a strong than linear increase of the relative J/\(\psi\) yield with respect to the relative multiplicity and reveals a significant \(p_{T}\) dependence. The \(p_{T}\) integrated J/\(\psi\) yield in events with a multiplicity of 4 times of the average charged-particle multiplicity is a factor of 25 respect to the average J/\(\psi\) yield. The results are compared with predications from PYTHIA8 simulation, The PYTHIA8 simulation underestimates the increase trend of J/\(\psi\) yields with respect to the charged-particle multiplicity at high multiplicities.

