#### Efficiency and acceptance correction

The embedding \(J/\psi\)\(p_{T}\) spectrum has a flat distribution, while the real J/\(\psi\) spectrum is apparently not a flat distribution. The momentum smearing effect will change the reconstructed J/\(\psi\) spectrum to a different distribution which shows an input J/\(\psi\)\(p_{T}\) shape dependence. However, it is the first time to measure the J/\(\psi\) cross section in the proton-proton collisions at \(\sqrt{s}\) = 500 GeV, the J/\(\psi\) transverse momentum spectrum is not known.

Figure 4.17: J/\(\psi\) total detector efficiency as a function of J/\(\psi\) transverse moemtnum.

To deal with this effect, an iteration method is used to get the final detection efficiency. First, a flat J/\(\psi\)\(p_{T}\) distribution is used as an input distribution, and one can obtain the J/\(\psi\) detection efficiency under this MC \(p_{T}\) shape. Second, the raw \(J/\psi\) spectrum is corrected by the efficiency getting from the first step. Third, the corrected \(J/\psi\) spectrum line shape is used as a new J/\(\psi\) input \(p_{T}\) spectrum. Then repeating the procedure until the final efficiency becomes a stable shape. The final \(J/\psi\) detection efficiency in the BHT1 trigger is shown in Figure 4.17. The curve turns on at about 4 GeV/\(c\). And the detector efficiency has a very rapidly increase at high \(p_{T}\) range. At the high-\(p_{T}\) range, the efficiency is more than 30%.

